============================================================
  ReadMe - Authentication Methods / SMS MFA Capability Audit
============================================================

PURPOSE
-------
This audit reports per-user authentication method configuration with a focus
on whether SMS can serve as a multifactor authentication (MFA) method. It is a
CONFIGURATION/CAPABILITY audit, not a usage audit. To prove a method was
actually used, validate against Microsoft Entra sign-in logs separately.

Data sources:
  - Microsoft Graph: Get-MgUser
  - Microsoft Graph: Get-MgUserAuthenticationMethod
  - Microsoft Graph: Get-MgUserAuthenticationPhoneMethod
  - Microsoft Graph (beta): /users/{id}/authentication/requirements
  - Microsoft Graph: Get-MgReportAuthenticationMethodUserRegistrationDetail
    (or beta /reports/authenticationMethods/userRegistrationDetails/{id} for targeted)
  - Microsoft Graph (beta): authenticationMethodConfigurations/Sms (tenant policy, queried once per run)

Required scopes:
  User.Read.All, UserAuthenticationMethod.Read.All, AuditLog.Read.All, Policy.Read.All


============================================================
COLUMN-BY-COLUMN VALIDATION
============================================================

UserPrincipalName
  Source : Get-MgUser
  Meaning: The user's UPN.
  Notes  : Primary identifier for each row.

Email
  Source : Get-MgUser (Mail)
  Meaning: The user's primary email address (if populated).
  Notes  : May be blank if no Mail attribute is set.

AccountEnabled
  Source : Get-MgUser (AccountEnabled)
  Meaning: Whether the account is enabled (True) or disabled (False).
  Notes  : "Enabled accounts" scope filters to AccountEnabled = True.

Domain
  Source : Derived from UserPrincipalName
  Meaning: The UPN domain suffix (text after the @).
  Notes  : Parsed locally; not a separate Graph call.

PerUserMfaState
  Source : beta /users/{id}/authentication/requirements (perUserMfaState)
  Meaning: Legacy per-user MFA state (disabled / enabled / enforced).
  Notes  : This is the legacy per-user MFA control, NOT Conditional Access.
           Returns 'Unavailable' if the value cannot be read.

IsMfaRegistered
  Source : Authentication methods registration report (isMfaRegistered)
  Meaning: User has registered at least one MFA method.
  Notes  : Reflects registration, not whether MFA is enforced.

IsMfaCapable
  Source : Authentication methods registration report (isMfaCapable)
  Meaning: User is MFA-capable per the EFFECTIVE authentication policy.
  Notes  : Microsoft-computed value. It already accounts for whether a
           registered method is enabled by policy, evaluating BOTH the legacy
           MFA policy and the modern Authentication methods policy. Because of
           this, it correctly covers users registered via either the
           pre-combined (legacy) or post-combined (converged) registration
           experience.

TenantSmsPolicyState
  Source : beta authenticationMethodConfigurations/Sms (state) - queried ONCE per run
  Meaning: Tenant-level SMS authentication method policy state (enabled / disabled).
  Notes  : This reads the MODERN Authentication methods policy. If SMS is
           scoped to specific GROUPS rather than all users, this tenant-level
           state can OVERSTATE per-user availability. For exact per-user
           evaluation, the policy includeTargets would need to be resolved
           against group membership.

SmsMethodRegistered
  Source : Live phone methods (mobile) OR registration report methodsRegistered
           (patterns: mobilePhone / sms / phone)
  Meaning: A mobile/SMS-capable phone method is registered for the user.
  Notes  : Uses both the live phone method object and the registration report,
           so it covers numbers registered through either the legacy or the
           combined registration experience.

SmsMfaAvailable
  Source : Derived (composite)
  Meaning: Best-effort indicator that SMS can serve as an MFA method for the user.
  Logic  : IsMfaCapable = True
           AND SmsMethodRegistered = True
           AND TenantSmsPolicyState = enabled
  Notes  : This is a CAPABILITY signal, NOT proof that SMS was used. To confirm
           SMS was actually used as MFA, cross-reference the Microsoft Entra
           sign-in logs (authenticationMethodsUsed / authentication details).
           Also subject to the group-scoping caveat noted under TenantSmsPolicyState.

SecurityQuestionsRegistered
  Source : securityQuestionAuthenticationMethod object
           OR registration report methodsRegistered (security question patterns)
  Meaning: Whether the user has security questions registered.
  Notes  : This is NOT inferred from IsSsprRegistered. SSPR registration alone
           does not prove security questions are one of the registered methods.

AllRegisteredAuthMethods
  Source : Get-MgUserAuthenticationMethod + normalized phone methods
  Meaning: Human-readable list of the user's registered authentication methods.
  Notes  : Phone methods are normalized to friendly labels
           (e.g., "Phone call (mobile)", "SMS").

PhoneType
  Source : Get-MgUserAuthenticationPhoneMethod (PhoneType / method ID mapping)
  Meaning: The phone type for the row (mobile / alternateMobile / office / None).
  Notes  : One row is emitted per registered phone method. Users with no phone
           methods produce a single row with PhoneType = None.

PhoneNumber
  Source : Get-MgUserAuthenticationPhoneMethod (PhoneNumber)
  Meaning: The registered phone number for that phone method.
  Notes  : May be masked depending on the calling role's permissions
           (e.g., Authentication Administrator sees masked numbers).


============================================================
KEY CAVEATS
============================================================

1. CAPABILITY vs USAGE
   SmsMfaAvailable indicates the user CAN use SMS for MFA based on
   capability + registration + tenant policy. It does NOT prove SMS was
   actually used. Use the Microsoft Entra sign-in logs to confirm real usage.

2. TENANT POLICY SCOPING
   TenantSmsPolicyState (and therefore SmsMfaAvailable) is based on the
   tenant-level SMS method policy state. If SMS is enabled only for specific
   groups, the tenant-level value may overstate availability for users who are
   not in those groups.

3. PRE- vs POST-COMBINED REGISTRATION
   IsMfaCapable is a Microsoft-computed value that accounts for both the legacy
   MFA policy and the modern Authentication methods policy, so it remains valid
   whether or not the tenant has migrated to the converged (combined)
   registration experience. Per-user registration (phone methods, security
   questions, etc.) is read from the unified authentication method store, which
   also reflects both registration experiences.

4. SMS SIGN-IN vs SMS MFA
   This audit intentionally does NOT report smsSignInState. That property
   reflects FIRST-FACTOR SMS sign-in readiness (passwordless phone sign-in),
   which is a different feature from using SMS as an MFA second factor. A user
   can use SMS for MFA even when smsSignInState is notAllowedByPolicy or
   notSupported.

============================================================
